Z||000007||Eric||11-01-1999||09:03 AM||||I heard people talking about faux carbon fiber dash kits for less than $100.  I didn't pay much attention because I already had mine.  Maybe try a search on this forum.  <P>I should be able to get some pictures of the dash/interior within the next two weeks.  Now I just have to figure out how to post them.<P>I didn't do any structural modifications to my dash or interior, but it does kind of look like a whole new car inside.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ggarsk||11-01-1999||11:02 AM||||Dupli-Color makes a vinyl and fabric paint. the dark charcoal is almost a identical match to the fieros dark gray. I did all my molding and trim with it. The interior looks new. If your interior is grey and your dash isn't in that good of shape I might be able to get you a dash that is in excellent shape.Let me know and I'll see if it's still there.<p>[This message has been edited by ggarsk (edited 11-01-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bill Osmer||11-01-1999||12:12 PM||||I just recently replaced the headliner in my 86 GT. It wasn't a tough job at all, and it made the drooping interior look like new again. Material is available from your local fabric stores (nylon foam-backed tricot) that's exactly the material to use (the medium gray is almost a perfect match). The adhesive choice is important - based on another thread, I learned about 3M's spray adhesive (version 90, I think) that's easy to use and is rated heavy duty. It was a couple hour job, with a dry time of over-nite. Well worth pursuing...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||11-01-1999||12:25 PM||||3M makes FoamFast 74 which is a spray adhesive for foam backed material.  It sprays on like crazy string and sets in about ten minutes.  That gives plenty of time to spray the headliner panel and the back of the fabric and work out any wrinkles.  In the meantime you can get Upholstry Twist Pins at walmart for around $1.79 per pack of ten.  They look like pushpins but the metal pin is twisted like a corkscrew.  You can spraypaint the plastic button part to match the headliner and then screw them into the headliner to hold the fabric up.  Position them to look like rolled and tucked interior.  This is a cheap and quick fix while you get the nerve to do it right.<P>I found interior spray paint that matches my 85 SE light grey interior exactly.  It is Warm Grey and is put out by SEM.  I got them to send me a catalog which shows all of their colors.<BR>You can use their #15303 Graphite Grey to re color the speaker grills on the grey dash.  There is a particular procedure for recoloring fabric that is outlined in the literature.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Carrolles||11-01-1999||12:26 PM||||Clarification:<BR>The Upholstry twist pins have a top that looks like a button, not a barrel like a push pin.||||reg||1
Z||000012||DXR_DAD||11-02-1999||10:00 PM||||This is the best glue I've ever used! made by LOCTITE  Super Bonder 409 Industrial Grade Gel "Instant Adhesive"  part # 40945. I got it from Grainger larger tube than super glue. <BR>works great on the padding/console||||reg||14
Z||000013||Greg Piet||11-04-1999||10:15 PM||||I got the nylon tricot med grey from jc whitney as per the description in teh fiero online service guide.  I go the material and it looks darn close!<BR>Where would I get one of these glues (3M 90) or whatever to attach to headliner board?<P>Right now I was going to go to an upholstry shop, but too lazy, and if there is stuff at a home depot or something I am more inclined to go there.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Carrolles||11-05-1999||07:25 AM||||I got both the fabric and the glue at Hancock Fabric Store in Birmingham, AL.  hancock is a Franchise chain.  I've also seen the glue at a trim supply store in downtown Birmingham.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Bill Osmer||11-05-1999||08:20 AM||||Greg -<P>I was able to find the 3M 90 adhesive at both Home Depot and Menard's (amazingly, not at auto parts stores!) BTW - I haven't noticed any residual smells, either.<P>I'm still pleased with my installation. Now, only time will be the real test of how good a job I did. <p>[This message has been edited by Bill Osmer (edited 11-05-1999).]||||reg||1
